Adopt the engine spelling in NoImplicitConversion: 22 of 22 measured engine messages name the target as a reference and end with a full stop, our text does neither


## MAX'S CALL, not the lane's — measured by B155 and deliberately not decided there

## The measurement

Across both corpora carrying `NoImplicitConversion` (b071 enum targets, b144
funcdef-handle targets): **22 of 22 measured engine messages name the target as a
reference AND end with a full stop.** Zero exceptions.

Counting only MEASURED columns — every unsuffixed spelling in those files lives
in comment prose, never in a measurement.

**There are TWO marks diverging, not one.** Chasing only the `&` would have fixed
half a divergence:

    engine (measured):  ... to 'VirtualKey&'.
    ours:               ... to 'VirtualKey'

## The case FOR adopting the engine spelling

The CLI's own footer asks users to report differences from Openplanet's output,
which makes exact-match close to a product commitment. And 22/22 uniformity means
there is **no per-case judgement** — it is one mechanical change.

## The case AGAINST, which is real

`VirtualKey&` leaks how the engine models assignment and is **not something a
user can write**. `to 'VirtualKey'` may genuinely read better to a human who is
trying to fix their code rather than match a string.

## Cost, fully costed by B155

- **One renderer line**, plus the documented transform.
- **15 exact-string assertions** are the known remainder: 12 in
  `b071_enum_type_mismatch`, 3 in `cli_check_tests`. Plus one suffix-insensitive
  `contains` in b110 that needs no change.
- All three `NoImplicitConversion` construction sites are initialisation or
  assignment positions (GH #49 math types, B071 enums, B144 funcdef binding), so
  **there is no site where a target `&` would be wrong.**

## Why this is cheap either way now

B155 did not decide it — it **pinned** it. The engine's side is derived from the
corpora (comment lines skipped), and a test converts each engine row by the
documented transform (drop the trailing `.`, drop the target's `&`) and requires
it to equal our text character-for-character. **If either side moves, it fails
and NAMES the new difference** rather than leaving a comment to rot.

That is the state the repo was previously missing. It had stated BOTH sides:
`checker.rs` said the unsuffixed spelling was deliberate, while
`b071_enum_type_mismatch.rs` said *"the engine's wording is part of the
requirement, not an implementation detail"* — and quoted `to 'LocalTwo'.`, **a
third spelling matching neither the engine (no `&`) nor our output (it has the
full stop).** That header is corrected.

## If adopted

Change the renderer, update the 15 assertions, and **invert the transform in
B155's test** so it keeps checking the relationship rather than being deleted.
The test is the durable asset here; the decision is one line.